+# Decision: Self-hosted socket delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard needs committed board changes to reach connected browsers. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one authoritative event revision; the open question was the route from commit to browser. Up to 800 staff browsers per market, bursts of 20 edits per second around opening time, and a goal of showing committed changes within 15 seconds (fresher is welcome) all shape the choice. It matters because the route determines how three unwritten components — the change store, delivery, and browser sync — are designed, and because the board's update path is the product's core loop: getting it wrong either costs money every event month or costs operations staff their weekends.
+
+This decision builds on the command-boundary decision recorded in the HLD (`Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event`): whichever route is chosen carries already-committed, already-revisioned events, and none of the options may create a second authority for board state.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Meets the 15-second freshness goal.** All three options pass; stated here only because it bounds how much latency any option may buy.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Update latency under normal connectivity** — *major*. The board's purpose is coordinators seeing stall status promptly; seconds of per-change lag compound across a shift.
+- **Recurring operating cost** — *major*. Forty markets run continuously through event hours; per-event or per-connection vendor fees scale with the project's success.
+- **Vendor coupling on the core update path** — *major*. If the realtime route belongs to a provider, its subscription model, quotas, and outages become the board's outages. This criterion cuts against the cheapest-to-operate option, which is why it is named rather than folded into cost.
+- **Owned operational burden** — *moderate*. Connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnection behavior must live somewhere; self-hosting moves that burden in-house.
+- **Read load amplification** — *moderate*. Read-heavy delivery multiplies load on the authoritative store at browser scale.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Every connected browser polls an HTTP endpoint every five seconds, passing a cursor (last revision seen) and receiving revisions committed since. Delivery is plain request/response — no persistent connections, no session state on the server. The change store needs an index that answers "revisions after cursor X for event Y" efficiently; browsers run a simple loop and treat a failed poll as a retry. There is no reconnect logic distinct from the polling loop itself.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Meets the 15-second goal: **passes** — worst-case lag is the poll interval plus processing, around five seconds.
+- Update latency: **weak** — every change waits up to a full interval, and 800 browsers polling every five seconds is a steady 160 requests/second of read load even when nothing changed.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor and no new infrastructure; it rides the existing HTTP tier.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — none.
+- Owned operational burden: **strong** — the least of the three; stateless request/response.
+- Read load amplification: **weak** — this is the option's defining cost: reads are repeated whether or not anything changed, and the store must still build and maintain cursor-query indexes.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest to operate, but pays in perpetual lag and in read volume that scales with browser count rather than change rate.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server holds persistent WebSocket connections to browsers and pushes each committed revision as it lands. Delivery owns connection lifecycle: authenticating sockets, tracking which browser watches which event, and tearing down dead connections. The change store must publish reliably — a committed revision must reach delivery even across restarts, so publication needs an outbox-style guarantee — and must keep revision history replayable, so a browser can request "everything after revision N" on reconnect. Browser sync owns reconnection with backoff and catch-up from the replayable history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Meets the 15-second goal: **passes** — push delivery, subsecond in practice.
+- Update latency: **strong** — a change reaches browsers as soon as it commits; no polling interval to wait out.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor; cost is the connection capacity already being paid for.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — none; the update path is entirely self-owned.
+- Owned operational burden: **weak** — this is the option's defining cost: connection capacity sizing for 800 browsers, session lifecycle, and reconnect semantics are all this project's problems now.
+- Read load amplification: **strong** — reads happen once per commit plus one replay per reconnecting browser, not per browser per interval.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates with no vendor in the critical path, bought with real connection and reconnection operations work distributed across three components.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider (pub/sub over websockets, channels per event) holds the browser connections. The server publishes each committed revision to the provider's channel API; browsers subscribe using the provider's client SDK. Delivery thins to an adapter around the provider; the change store must reconcile publication with the provider — a publish call can fail or be accepted-but-dropped, so the store keeps its own revision history as the recovery authority and re-publishes on divergence.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Meets the 15-second goal: **passes** — push delivery via the provider.
+- Update latency: **strong** — comparable to self-hosted sockets in the happy path.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — per-connection or per-message fees recur every event month and scale with success.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — the board's core update path runs through the provider's SDK, quotas, and status page; divergence recovery exists precisely because the provider is a second authority on "was this revision delivered."
+- Owned operational burden: **strong** — connection ops largely outsourced; the price is reconciliation logic instead.
+- Read load amplification: **strong** — no polling reads.
+
+**Summary.** Quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection ops, bought with recurring fees and a provider coupled into the product's most load-bearing path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets. The two push options tie on latency and read load; the decision turns on the *major* criteria that separate them. Recurring cost and vendor coupling both weigh against the hosted service, and the coupling is not incidental: putting a provider between commit and browser contradicts the tenet that **a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route** in spirit — the provider becomes a second component whose state determines whether a revision actually arrives. The project's tenet that **self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted** means this lean is a choice, not an inherited constraint, and it was made deliberately: the connection and reconnection burden is accepted as the price of keeping the board's core loop vendor-free.
+
+Committing forecloses nothing structural — polling or a hosted provider could still be adopted later for specific routes without violating the command-boundary decision — but it obligates the three downstream nodes: the change store must treat publication as a reliability problem (replayable revisions, no lost revisions between commit and socket delivery), delivery must own session lifecycle as a first-class concern, and browser sync must make disconnection visible and recovery automatic. The **connectivity failures must be visible** tenet binds all three: a dropped socket must never present as a quiet board.
